{"duration_api_ms":114416,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"a7e13f19-00a9-4c23-b475-6f6de0a38c24","total_cost_usd":0.3294612,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15219,"cache_read_input_tokens":189611,"output_tokens":10769,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":7913},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15219},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":10769,"cacheReadInputTokens":189611,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15219,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3294612,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":7913,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":8,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The answer is in `/work/answer.md`. sol wrote it, I reviewed it line by line, and I've marked it FINAL on the board. argon had not posted a conclusion when I signed off.\n\n**Condition.** Assume all three single-item values are positive and v_P < V = v_A + v_B + v_C. The Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F exactly when one of these holds (boundaries included):\n- v_P ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C), or\n- v_P ≥ V − min(v_A, v_B, v_C), or\n- v_A = v_B = v_C.\n\nIf one of the three has value zero, F is a line segment or a single point, and the two points always coincide.\n\n**Proof outline.**\n1. **The face F.** The minimum core revenue is R = v_P. F is the set of payment vectors with total v_P where each payment lies between that bidder's VCG payment and their value.\n2. **Shift by the VCG payments.** In shifted coordinates F becomes a triangle with each coordinate capped at h_i = min(v_i, d), where d = V − v_P. The Vickrey-nearest point becomes the point of F closest to the origin.\n3. **Key step.** The centroid lies strictly inside F, so the two points coincide only if the centroid's three coordinates are equal. Swapping two coordinates shows that if h_i < h_j and the cap h_i actually cuts F, the centroid's i-coordinate is strictly smaller. So the points coincide exactly when all caps are equal or no cap cuts F. Rewritten in terms of the values, that gives the three cases above.\n\n**Example where they differ.** Take (v_A, v_B, v_C) = (1, 2, 3) and v_P = 2. VCG charges nothing, and F is the triangle p_A + p_B + p_C = 2 with p ≥ 0, with the corner beyond p_A = 1 cut off. The Vickrey-nearest point is (2/3, 2/3, 2/3), but the centroid is (4/9, 7/9, 7/9).\n\n**Checks.** I compared the exact polygon centroid with the nearest point on 20,000 random instances, and the condition held. The only two mismatches were rounding effects just at a boundary of the condition, where the gap between the points is tiny. sol also checked the condition exactly with fractions on 2,125 instances with integer values and found no exceptions. My numbers reproduce the example.\n\nThere is one cosmetic issue in `answer.md`: inline maths is written in plain parentheses instead of `\\( \\)`, so it may not render as maths. I didn't change it, since the file is shared.","ttft_ms":2429,"type":"result","duration_ms":212571,"uuid":"072bc33b-f74f-4be0-bafa-039ada3ee110","ttft_stream_ms":1598,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":1598,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
